{"subjectId":"c","id":"stdlib-tour","title":"Standard Library Tour: stdlib.h","group":"Standard Library, Modern C & Interview","order":53,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The C standard library is small — about 30 headers. <code>&lt;stdlib.h&gt;</code> is the grab-bag: memory, conversions, random numbers, sorting, searching, integer arithmetic and process control.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>The headers you will use</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Provides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&lt;stdio.h&gt;</code></td><td>Streams, <code>printf</code>/<code>scanf</code> families, files</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;stdlib.h&gt;</code></td><td><code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code>, <code>strtol</code>/<code>strtod</code>, <code>rand</code>, <code>qsort</code>/<code>bsearch</code>, <code>abs</code>/<code>div</code>, <code>exit</code>, <code>getenv</code>, <code>system</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td><code>str…</code> and <code>mem…</code> functions</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;ctype.h&gt;</code></td><td>Character classification and case conversion</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;math.h&gt;</code></td><td><code>sqrt</code>, <code>pow</code>, trigonometry, rounding (link with <code>-lm</code> on Linux)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;time.h&gt;</code></td><td><code>time</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>struct tm</code>, <code>strftime</code>, <code>mktime</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;stdint.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;inttypes.h&gt;</code></td><td>Fixed-width integers and their printf macros</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;stdbool.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;stddef.h&gt;</code></td><td><code>bool</code>; <code>size_t</code>, <code>NULL</code>, <code>offsetof</code>, <code>ptrdiff_t</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;limits.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;float.h&gt;</code></td><td>Ranges of the numeric types</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;errno.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;assert.h&gt;</code></td><td>Error numbers; debug assertions</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;stdarg.h&gt;</code></td><td>Variadic functions</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;signal.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;setjmp.h&gt;</code></td><td>Signals; non-local jumps</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;stdatomic.h&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;threads.h&gt;</code> (C11)</td><td>Atomics; threads (optional — often POSIX <code>pthread.h</code> instead)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Integer helpers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d %ld %lld\\n\", abs(-7), labs(-70000L), llabs(-9000000000LL));\n    div_t d = div(17, 5);                 /* quotient and remainder together */\n    printf(\"17 / 5 = %d r %d\\n\", d.quot, d.rem);\n    printf(\"%ld %lu %.3f\\n\", strtol(\"-0x1F\", NULL, 16), strtoul(\"777\", NULL, 8), strtod(\"6.02e23\", NULL) / 1e23);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7 70000 9000000000\n17 / 5 = 3 r 2\n-31 511 6.020</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-dice\"></i>Random numbers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint roll(void) { return rand() % 6 + 1; }              /* slight modulo bias; fine for games */\n\nint main(void) {\n    srand(42);                                          /* same seed -&gt; same sequence */\n    int first[5], ok = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) { first[i] = roll(); if (first[i] &lt; 1 || first[i] &gt; 6) ok = 0; }\n    srand(42);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) if (roll() != first[i]) ok = 0;\n    printf(\"rolls in 1..6 and reproducible: %s\\n\", ok ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n    printf(\"RAND_MAX &gt;= 32767: %s\\n\", RAND_MAX &gt;= 32767 ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n\n    double u = rand() / (RAND_MAX + 1.0);               /* uniform in [0, 1) */\n    printf(\"u in [0,1): %s\\n\", u &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; u &lt; 1 ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>rolls in 1..6 and reproducible: yes\nRAND_MAX &gt;= 32767: yes\nu in [0,1): yes</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Seed once with <code>srand((unsigned)time(NULL))</code> for different runs. <code>rand()</code> is low quality and <strong>never</strong> suitable for security — use the OS (<code>getrandom</code>, <code>arc4random</code>, <code>/dev/urandom</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>Process and environment</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nvoid goodbye(void) { printf(\"atexit handler ran\\n\"); }\n\nint main(void) {\n    atexit(goodbye);                     /* runs when main returns or exit() is called */\n    const char *path = getenv(\"PATH\");\n    printf(\"PATH is %s\\n\", path ? \"set\" : \"unset\");\n    printf(\"NO_SUCH_VAR -&gt; %s\\n\", getenv(\"NO_SUCH_VAR_123\") ? \"set\" : \"NULL\");\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>PATH is set\nNO_SUCH_VAR -&gt; NULL\natexit handler ran</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does exit() do that return from a function does not?</summary><div><p><code>exit</code> terminates the whole process from anywhere: it calls functions registered with <code>atexit</code> in reverse order, flushes and closes all open streams, removes <code>tmpfile</code> files, and returns the status to the OS. Returning from <code>main</code> does the same; returning from any other function just goes back to its caller.</p></div></details></section>"}